/retry now uses the tool name the prompt loop already records, and only falls back to scanning the error text when no name was recorded. All four checks pass: build, the full cargo test suite (0 failures, integration tests included), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check. The final commit is 8d1f0ff3. The two WIP commits before it were made before any cargo run, as the task asked.

The fix
• **The bug:** the prompt result already carried the real tool name, and auto-retry used it. But the REPL only kept the error text, so `/retry` guessed the tool by searching that text. An edit_file failure whose message mentioned "bash" got a bash recovery hint.
• **New helper, resolve_retry_tool(carried, error):** a non-empty carried name wins. If the name is missing or empty, it falls back to the old text search, and the result is identical to before.
• **Plumbing:** a new `last_error_tool` field sits next to `last_error` in the REPL state, `PostPromptContext` and `DispatchContext`. It is set at every place last_error is set, so a new error can never be paired with an old tool name:
  • after a prompt, fallback-provider retry, /spawn or /explain: taken from the prompt result, but only when that result actually recorded a tool error (a small error_tool_of helper does this);
  • after the watch-fix result, the two /plan clears and the /extended clear: set to None.
• `src/prompt.rs and src/dispatch_sub.rs` were not touched.

One change beyond the plan: the task said to set the name to None after /retry itself runs. Instead, handle_retry now returns the error and the tool name from the retried turn. Setting it to None would have been safe, but a second /retry would then have gone back to guessing the tool from the text.

Tests
• A table test in commands_retry.rs covers:
  • the bug: a carried edit_file beats error text that mentions bash;
  • no carried name: exactly the old search result;
  • an empty name: treated as no name;
  • neither a name nor an error: returns None.
• The fixture includes a check that the old search really does return bash on it, so the bug row can't pass by accident.
• A second test checks that a name is only carried alongside an actual tool error.
• **Positive control:** I made the helper ignore the carried name (break, test and restore in one command). Exactly the `edit_file`-vs-`bash` row failed by name; after restoring it passed and the tree was clean.

Docs: I added an ARCHITECTURE.md entry for commands_retry.rs. It records that the two earlier attempts (#773, #779) stalled because the REPL had no field to carry the name, not because the helper was hard. CLAUDE.md is unchanged. tests/module_size.rs passed, so no size entries needed re-registering.

Not verified: nothing drives the plumbing through a live REPL session. The tests cover the two helpers, and the new field is set at every site the task named (I listed them all with grep).
